# config/subsys/Kconfig.secure-boot — Secure boot and link provisioning enforcement
#
# Maps to cargo features `secure-boot-enforced` and
# `link-provisioning-enforced`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secure boot
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    # cargo-feature: secure-boot-enforced
    bool "Enforce Ed25519 signature verification at boot" if !BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if !BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default n
    help
      Default: y on every build except BUILD_TYPE_DEV (release-safe;
      the QEMU board is a development build, the boards are release
      builds). A development build keeps the TEST key and unsigned boot.
      When enabled, the kernel verifies the signature of the slot's image
      file early in its boot (kernel/src/boot/ota.rs), with the Ed25519
      public key embedded at build time (crates/core/ota). If it is absent
      or invalid, the next boot is pointed at the recovery slot and the
      board resets, when that slot verifies; otherwise boot halts.
      Disable only for development.  MUST be y for any production
      deployment — enforced by cargo feature `secure-boot-enforced`.
      A BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build has it on and cannot turn it off.

config LINK_PROVISIONING_ENFORCED
    # cargo-feature: link-provisioning-enforced
    bool "Brain link provisioning: no boot without a link key, no UART bridge" if !BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if !BUILD_TYPE_DEV
    default n
    depends on USE_ROBOT
    help
      Robot domain only (wave 11): the brain link exists only there.
      Default: y on every build except BUILD_TYPE_DEV (owner decision
      2026-10-23: the link fails closed), as SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED: the
      release robot images (vf2, k1, rpi5, robot-embedded, robot-fleet)
      need a provisioned link key, a development build keeps a keyless
      boot going with the link down. A BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build has it
      on and cannot turn it off. This symbol is the only source: no board
      cargo feature turns it on behind the .config's back.
      The brain's TCP link is always a Noise session (ChaCha20-Poly1305,
      keys from a per-connection handshake): there is no plaintext mode and
      no MAC-only mode in any build, and a key in CONFIG.INI cannot change
      that.  This option hardens what surrounds it:
        * boot halts when the link key (the reserved sector past the
          filesystem, which no file write can reach) is missing or invalid, instead
          of going on with the link down;
        * the handshake is refused while the entropy pool is unseeded, so
          no session is ever keyed from timing alone;
        * the UART bridge, which carries brain-protocol frames (including
          motion commands) outside any session, is down in both directions.
      A keyless encrypted-only robot can never talk, so the failure
      surfaces at provisioning, not in the field. The refusal is a `login`
      decision record (method 5, the brain link; reason 4, no key to
      verify with) on every keyless boot, whether this option halts it or
      not.
      Enforced by cargo feature `link-provisioning-enforced`, compile-time
      only, no runtime override, mirroring SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED.
      Note the trade-off: a unit that loses its key will not boot.
